-- title:
--   Linear disjointness descends finiteness over K₀(x)
-- statement:
--   Let $K_0$, $L$, $F_0$, $F$ be fields, with $F_0$ a $K_0$-algebra, $F$ an $L$-algebra and $F$ an $F_0$-algebra (no compatibility between these three structures is assumed beyond what is stated). Assume the linear disjointness hypothesis: for every natural number $n$ and every family $b \colon \{0,\dots,n-1\} \to F_0$ that is linearly independent over $K_0$, the family of images $i \mapsto \mathrm{algebraMap}_{F_0,F}(b\,i)$ is linearly independent over $L$. Let $x \in F_0$ be transcendental over $K_0$, i.e. the evaluation map $K_0[X] \to F_0$ at $x$ is injective, and write $x' = \mathrm{algebraMap}_{F_0,F}(x) \in F$. Assume further that $F$ is finite-dimensional as a module over the intermediate field $L\langle x'\rangle = L(x')$ of $F$ generated by $L$ and $x'$. The conclusion is that $F_0$ is finite-dimensional as a module over the intermediate field $K_0\langle x\rangle = K_0(x)$ of $F_0$ generated by $K_0$ and $x$.
--
--   This is the standard descent of finiteness along a linearly disjoint pair: finiteness of $F$ over $L(x')$ forces finiteness of $F_0$ over $K_0(x)$, with the implicit bound $[F_0 : K_0(x)] \le [F : L(x')]$. It serves in the function-field side of the curve machinery, where finiteness of a one-variable function field over a rational subfield is transported down from an enlarged constant field; it is used by several results of the node annulus development.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IntermediateField_finiteDimensional_adjoin_of_linearDisjoint_of_transcendental.lean

